{
  "dom_id": "step-q42682",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+2y-4z=-4 \\\\ x-2y+5z=-6 \\\\ 10x+y+2z=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that  $x=-\\frac{20}{3}$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $12-9k,$ find the value of $k.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=12-9k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(-4-5)-2(-12-40)-4(-6+16) \\\\  & =36+104-40 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{100}{12-9k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=-\\frac{20}{3}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{100}{12-9k} & =-\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ 15 & =-12+9k \\\\ 9k & =27 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
